Lines 83-95:
    final long contentLength = unsignedExtend(in.readInt());
    if(contentLength < Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
        final byte [] buffer = new byte[(int)contentLength];
        in.readFully(buffer);
        return buffer;
    } else {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Very long byte vectors and strings not currently supported");
    }
The guard excludes exactly one value (2147483647). A wire-supplied length of 0x7FFFFFFE passes it, so five bytes of input — the type tag plus the four length bytes — request a 2147483646-byte array before readFully() reads a single byte of the value. The size is never compared against the bytes actually remaining in the enclosing frame, and no configurable ceiling exists anywhere in the file. Three reachable entry points share the sink: the public readLongstr() (lines 100-112), the field-table tag 'S' (line 163) and the field-table tag 'x' (line 211), the last two nestable inside readTable/readArray to any depth.
